    kind: 'concept',
    title: 'States: state definitions, the current initial-state form, transitions, guards and effects',
    paragraphs: [
      'A state definition is like a state machine in UML and SysML v1: it defines a behavioural state that a system can exhibit, it can specify a set of discrete nested states, and the states are connected by transitions that fire on acceptance of item transfers, like accept actions (R73 sl.48). The initial state is written first start then off; — the slides record that this form replaced the earlier entry form in 2026-03 (sl.48–50). The 2.1 Beta 2 specification text still shows the earlier form, entry; then off; (S06 p.150–151), and the course’s probe found that the 2026-08 checker accepts both. The course uses the current form, as the weekly plan requires; if you meet the older form in a source, read it as the same statement.',
      'A transition is written transition name first source accept Trigger if guard do effect then target; and has a shorthand that omits the name and takes the lexically previous state as its source (sl.48–49). The guard is a condition that must be true for the transition to fire; the effect action is performed when the transition fires, before entry to the target (sl.53). Besides accepting a transfer, a transition can fire on a change trigger (accept when a Boolean expression becomes true), an absolute time trigger (accept at an instant) or a relative time trigger (accept after a duration) (sl.54). A state can have entry, do and exit actions, either referencing an action defined elsewhere or defining one in place, and states, like actions, can have parameters (sl.52). When a transition fires, the source’s do action is interrupted, then its exit action, the effect and the target’s entry action are performed in that order, and then the target’s do action starts (S06 p.153–154). S06 is an extension reading, so this paragraph is the course’s statement of that order: the quiz relies on it, not on reading R82.',
      'Two structural rules matter for AeroLink. A parallel state has concurrent nested states, and transitions between those concurrent states are not allowed (sl.51). In a non-parallel state with substates, exactly one substate is active at any time after entry (S06 p.146). A state definition is a kind of action definition (S06 p.145), which is why a state can do actions and a part can exhibit it the way it performs an action. exhibit identifies the part that exhibits states defined elsewhere, and parameters of the exhibited state are bound as for an action (sl.56).',
    ],
    citations: [{ reading: 'R73', pages: '48–56' }, { reading: 'R82', pages: '145–146' }, { reading: 'R82', pages: '150–151' }, { reading: 'R82', pages: '153–154' }],
