      'SEBoK notes that reviews can be schedule-based, event-based or evidence-based, each with its own risk — insufficient information, insufficient analysis, or late feedback on latent defects — and that a review team works best when it combines people close enough to the project to see what matters with people far enough away to be independent (R06c p.387–388). An asynchronous written review of a model by a peer team is an event-based review with an independent reviewer, which is why the course pairs it with an instructor review.',
      'For an operator such as AeroLink (instructor application), behaviour models earn their keep where documents struggle: an operations manual describes the weather-hold procedure, but only a model can be checked mechanically for an unreachable state or a branch that never ends, and only a model makes the link from “hold longer than 2 h” to the function and the element responsible for cancelling bookings explicit enough for a reviewer to test it. The same discipline carries into later life-cycle reviews: findings that name a location, a criterion and a fix can be dispositioned, tracked and closed; findings that do not are re-discovered at the next review.',
